## 🎯 Quick Reference

### File Structure
```
src/app/
├── components/
│   ├── common/          # Reusable UI components
│   ├── core/            # Feature-specific components  
│   └── layout/          # Application shell components
├── services/            # Business logic and API services
├── guards/              # Route protection and access control
├── pipes/               # Data transformation pipes
├── directives/          # Custom Angular directives
└── models/              # TypeScript interfaces and types
```

### Key Technologies
- **Angular 16** with TypeScript strict mode
- **Bulma CSS** for styling and layout
- **RxJS** for reactive state management
- **Chart.js** for data visualization
- **Rete.js** for node-based visual editing
- **Cypress** for end-to-end testing

### Essential Patterns

#### Component Structure
```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-component-name',
  templateUrl: './component-name.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./component-name.component.css']
})
export class ComponentNameComponent implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  private destroySubject = new Subject<void>();
  
  ngOnInit() {
    // Setup subscriptions with takeUntil pattern
  }
  
  ngOnDestroy() {
    this.destroySubject.next();
    this.destroySubject.complete();
  }
}
```

#### Service Pattern
```typescript
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class ServiceName {
  private dataSubject = new BehaviorSubject<DataType[]>([]);
  public data$ = this.dataSubject.asObservable();
  
  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
}
```

#### State Management
```typescript
// Use SharedService for global state
constructor(private sharedService: SharedService) {}

ngOnInit() {
  this.sharedService.userState$
    .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroySubject))
    .subscribe(state => {
      // Handle state changes
    });
}
```

## 🔧 Development Workflow

### Getting Started
1. Follow [Project Architecture](mdc:project-architecture.mdc) for understanding the codebase
2. Use [Component Guidelines](mdc:component-guidelines.mdc) when creating new components
3. Apply [Service Patterns](mdc:service-patterns.mdc) for business logic
4. Follow [Code Style](mdc:code-style.mdc) for consistent formatting
5. Implement [Testing Conventions](mdc:testing-conventions.mdc) for quality assurance

### Common Tasks

#### Creating a New Component
1. Use Angular CLI: `ng generate component feature/component-name`
2. Follow the component structure from [Component Guidelines](mdc:component-guidelines.mdc)
3. Implement proper lifecycle management with destroy pattern
4. Add unit tests following [Testing Conventions](mdc:testing-conventions.mdc)

#### Adding a New Service
1. Use Angular CLI: `ng generate service services/service-name`
2. Follow service patterns from [Service Patterns](mdc:service-patterns.mdc)
3. Use BehaviorSubjects for state management
4. Implement proper error handling and HTTP interceptors

#### Styling Components
1. Use Bulma CSS classes as primary styling approach
3. Use CSS variables for theming
4. Ensure responsive design for mobile compatibility

## 🚀 Build & Deployment

### Development Commands
```bash
yarn install           # Install dependencies
yarn start             # Development server (http://localhost:4200)
yarn build             # Production build
yarn test              # Unit tests with Karma
yarn e2e               # End-to-end tests with Cypress
yarn lint              # ESLint code analysis
yarn clean             # Clean dist and node_modules
yarn watch             # Development build with watch mode

# Development server with custom host/port
yarn start --host 0.0.0.0 --port 4200

# Production build using build script
./build --clean-start  # Production build with clean start
```

### Quality Gates
- Unit test coverage: 80% minimum
- E2E test coverage for critical user flows
- ESLint passing with zero errors

